<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sibylla, Queen of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1160–<a href="/years/1190">1190</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Queen Sibylla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sibylla was Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1186 to 1190, daughter of Amalric I and sister of the leper king Baldwin IV. Against much of the nobility she had her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> crowned with her, and their reign saw the disastrous defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> and the loss of Jerusalem to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1187. She died of illness in 1190 during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, leaving the succession to her half-sister Isabella I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sibylla-queen-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Sibylla, Queen of Jerusalem" width="138" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queen_Sibyl_of_Jerusalem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sibylla was the elder child of Amalric, then count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, and his first wife Agnes of Courtenay. When Baldwin III died in 1163 the High Court forced Amalric to annul the marriage as a condition of accepting him as king, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-iii">Pope Alexander III</a> declared the children legitimate.</p><p>Amalric died on 11 July 1174. His son Baldwin IV, aged 13, was chosen king despite fears of leprosy, and Sibylla became his heir presumptive. The High Court and the regent, Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a>, arranged her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-longsword">William Longsword</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> in 1176. William died in June 1177 while she was pregnant, leaving her countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, and in the winter of 1177–78 she gave birth to a son, Baldwin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1180 Baldwin IV arranged her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a>, a Poitevin knight. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> reports that Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and Bohemond III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> were marching on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to stage a coup, and the historian Bernard Hamilton concludes that the quick marriage foiled their plan. It divided the nobility into factions for and against Guy. In 1183 the king, by then blind and unable to walk unaided, had Sibylla's five-year-old son crowned co-king as Baldwin V and tried to have her marriage annulled, but she and Guy refused to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>.</p><p>Baldwin V died in August 1186. Sibylla secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and gained the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>, Gerard of Ridefort and the patriarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>. Her backers required her to leave Guy in return for recognition, and she agreed on condition that she could choose her next husband. At her coronation, probably in mid-September, the patriarch gave her a second crown and asked her to name a consort, and she crowned Guy herself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> invaded in April 1187. Guy was captured at the Horns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> on 4 July, and Sibylla commanded the defence of Jerusalem until it surrendered. She won Guy's release in July 1188.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a> refused to admit the couple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Sibylla accompanied Guy to the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>, and an epidemic struck the camp in 1190. She died in late summer or autumn, with her daughters Alice and Maria. Her half-sister Isabella I succeeded her.</p><p>Hamilton rejects the chronicler Ernoul's portrait of her as fickle and foolish, saying that it bears little relation to the known facts. Roger of Wendover called her a most praiseworthy woman, and the historian Helen J. Nicholson attributes the distortion of her image to contemporary gender ideals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sibylla of Jerusalem” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The crown passed several times through women: Melisende, daughter of Baldwin II, ruled with her husband Fulk of Anjou from 1131 and remained powerful after his death, and the claims of <b>Sibylla</b>, Isabella I and Isabella II brought in husbands from the West.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Acre, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1179" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sibylla, Queen of Jerusalem"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1179">What was here in 1179? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
